.nh
.SH NAME
ktm-guide-methods \- Methods (chapter 13 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A method is a function that belongs to a struct: it is named \fBType.name\fR, written outside the struct, and called on a value with \fBvalue.name(...)\fR\&. A struct can also extend one other struct and get its fields and methods. This chapter covers defining and calling methods, \fBself\fR, the conventions for making and freeing values, inheritance, hiding a method, and why there is no virtual dispatch (and what to do instead). ktm has no classes and no constructors: a struct is data, and its methods are functions.
.SH Defining a method
.PP
A method is written like a function whose name is the struct's name, a dot, and the method's name. Inside it, \fBself\fR is a pointer to the value it was called on.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Account {
    u8 *owner;
    i64 balance;
};

void Account.deposit(i64 amount) {
    self.balance += amount;
}

void Account.print() {
    printf("%s: %d\en", self.owner, self.balance);
}

i32 main() {
    Account a = {"ann", 0};
    a.deposit(100);
    a.deposit(25);
    a.print();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
ann: 125
.EE
.RE
.PP
Because \fBself\fR is a pointer, a method changes the caller's value, not a copy: \fBa.deposit(100)\fR changed \fBa\fR\&.
.PP
A method can return a value like any function:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Account {
    u8 *owner;
    i64 balance;
};

bool Account.withdraw(i64 amount) {
    if (amount > self.balance) {
        return false;
    }
    self.balance \-= amount;
    return true;
}

i32 main() {
    Account a = {"ann", 100};
    printf("%d %d\en", a.withdraw(30), a.withdraw(500));
    printf("%d\en", a.balance);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 0
70
.EE
.RE
.SH Calling methods
.PP
A method is called on a struct value or on a pointer to one, the same way:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Counter { i64 n; };

void Counter.bump() {
    self.n++;
}

i32 main() {
    Counter c = {0};
    Counter *p = &c;
    c.bump();
    p.bump();
    p\->bump();
    printf("%d\en", c.n);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3
.EE
.RE
.PP
It can be called on an element of an array, on a field, or on a struct a function just returned:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x, y; };

i64 Point.sum() {
    return self.x + self.y;
}

struct Segment { Point a, b; };

Point make(i64 x, i64 y) {
    Point p = {x, y};
    return p;
}

i32 main() {
    Point ps[2] = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
    Segment s = {{5, 6}, {7, 8}};
    printf("%d %d %d\en", ps[1].sum(), s.b.sum(), make(10, 20).sum());
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7 15 30
.EE
.RE
.PP
A method that returns \fBself\fR lets calls be chained:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Builder { i64 total; };

Builder *Builder.add(i64 n) {
    self.total += n;
    return self;
}

i32 main() {
    Builder b = {0};
    b.add(1).add(2).add(3);
    printf("%d\en", b.total);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
6
.EE
.RE
.PP
Methods take everything a function takes: default arguments, overloads (see Overloading (see ktm-guide-overloading(7))) and variable arguments (see Varargs (see ktm-guide-varargs(7))).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Door { bool open; i64 angle; };

void Door.swing(i64 angle = 90) {
    self.open = angle > 0;
    self.angle = angle;
}

i32 main() {
    Door d;
    d.swing();
    printf("%d %d\en", d.open, d.angle);
    d.swing(0);
    printf("%d %d\en", d.open, d.angle);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 90
0 0
.EE
.RE
.SH \fBself\fR
.PP
\fBself\fR is a pointer to the value; its fields are reached with \fBself.field\fR\&. It is fixed for the whole call: assigning to it is an error.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x; };

void Point.reset() {
    self = null;
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
cannot assign to const 'self'
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB*self\fR is the value itself, for when a copy is wanted:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x, y; };

Point Point.mirrored() {
    Point m = *self;
    m.x = \-m.x;
    return m;
}

i32 main() {
    Point p = {3, 4};
    Point q = p.mirrored();
    printf("(%d, %d) (%d, %d)\en", p.x, p.y, q.x, q.y);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
(3, 4) (\-3, 4)
.EE
.RE
.PP
A method calls the struct's other methods through \fBself\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Temp { f64 celsius; };

f64 Temp.fahrenheit() {
    return self.celsius * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0;
}

void Temp.print() {
    printf("%.1f C = %.1f F\en", self.celsius, self.fahrenheit());
}

i32 main() {
    Temp t = {21.5};
    t.print();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
21.5 C = 70.7 F
.EE
.RE
.SH Declaring a method ahead
.PP
A method's prototype, \fBType.name(...);\fR without a body, lets it be called before it is defined:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x; };

void Point.show();

i32 main() {
    Point a = {4};
    a.show();
    return 0;
}

void Point.show() {
    printf("%d\en", self.x);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4
.EE
.RE
.SH Making and freeing values
.PP
There are no constructors or destructors. By convention a plain function makes the value (often \fBnew\fR or \fBopen\fR in a module, see Modules and packages (see ktm-guide-modules-and-packages(7))), and a \fBfree()\fR method releases what it holds:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

struct Buffer {
    u8 *data;
    i64 len;
};

Buffer bufferNew(i64 size) {
    Buffer b = {mem.allocZero(size), size};
    return b;
}

void Buffer.free() {
    mem.free(self.data);
    self.data = null;
    printf("freed %d bytes\en", self.len);
}

i32 main() {
    Buffer b = bufferNew(64);
    b.data[0] = 'x';
    printf("%c\en", b.data[0]);
    b.free();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
x
freed 64 bytes
.EE
.RE
.PP
A value with a \fBfree()\fR method can be declared \fBscoped\fR instead of a type's plain declaration: \fBfree()\fR is then called for it when its block ends, however the block is left (see defer and scoped (see ktm-guide-defer-and-scoped(7))).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

struct Buffer {
    u8 *data;
    i64 len;
};

Buffer bufferNew(i64 size) {
    Buffer b = {mem.allocZero(size), size};
    return b;
}

void Buffer.free() {
    mem.free(self.data);
    printf("freed %d bytes\en", self.len);
}

i32 main() {
    scoped Buffer b = bufferNew(16);
    b.data[0] = 'y';
    printf("%c\en", b.data[0]);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
y
freed 16 bytes
.EE
.RE
.PP
A method that sets fields up can stand in for a constructor too, called on a zeroed value:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Range { i64 lo, hi, step; };

void Range.init(i64 lo, i64 hi, i64 step = 1) {
    self.lo = lo;
    self.hi = hi;
    self.step = step;
}

i64 Range.count() {
    return (self.hi \- self.lo + self.step \- 1) / self.step;
}

i32 main() {
    Range r;
    r.init(0, 10, 3);
    printf("%d\en", r.count());
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4
.EE
.RE
.SH Inheritance
.PP
\fBstruct Derived : Base { ... };\fR makes a struct that starts with all of \fBBase\fR\&'s fields, followed by its own. It also has \fBBase\fR\&'s methods.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct User {
    u8 *name;
    i64 age;
};

struct Admin : User {
    i64 level;
};

void User.describe() {
    printf("%s is %d\en", self.name, self.age);
}

void User.birthday() {
    self.age++;
}

i32 main() {
    Admin a;
    a.name = "bob";
    a.age = 40;
    a.level = 2;
    a.birthday();
    a.describe();
    printf("%d bytes\en", sizeof(Admin));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
bob is 41
24 bytes
.EE
.RE
.PP
Because a derived struct begins with its base, a pointer to it can be used wherever a pointer to the base is expected:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct User { u8 *name; i64 age; };
struct Admin : User { i64 level; };

void greet(User *u) {
    printf("hello %s\en", u.name);
}

i32 main() {
    Admin a;
    a.name = "bob";
    a.level = 3;
    greet(&a);
    User *u = &a;
    printf("%s\en", u.name);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
hello bob
bob
.EE
.RE
.PP
Going the other way, from a base pointer to a derived one, is a plain conversion: it is only right when the value really is the derived struct.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct User { u8 *name; };
struct Admin : User { i64 level; };

void show(User *u, bool isAdmin) {
    if (isAdmin) {
        Admin *a = u;
        printf("%s, level %d\en", a.name, a.level);
    } else {
        printf("%s\en", u.name);
    }
}

i32 main() {
    Admin a;
    a.name = "bob";
    a.level = 3;
    User u = {"ann"};
    show(&a, true);
    show(&u, false);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
bob, level 3
ann
.EE
.RE
.PP
The chain can go on: a struct can extend one that extends another.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Animal { u8 *name; };
struct Dog : Animal { i64 tricks; };
struct Puppy : Dog { i64 ageWeeks; };

void Animal.hello() {
    printf("I am %s\en", self.name);
}

i32 main() {
    Puppy p;
    p.name = "rex";
    p.tricks = 2;
    p.ageWeeks = 9;
    p.hello();
    printf("%d %d, %d bytes\en", p.tricks, p.ageWeeks, sizeof(Puppy));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
I am rex
2 9, 24 bytes
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A struct extends at most one base.
.IP \(bu 2
The base's fields come first, in its layout; the derived struct's follow.
.IP \(bu 2
A derived struct initialised with \fB{...}\fR takes the base's fields first, then its own.
.SH Hiding a base method
.PP
A method on the derived struct with the same name as the base's hides it. \fBBase.name(self)\fR still calls the base's version, for a method that adds to it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct User { u8 *name; };
struct Admin : User { i64 level; };

void User.describe() {
    printf("user %s\en", self.name);
}

void Admin.describe() {
    printf("admin %d, ", self.level);
    User.describe(self);
}

i32 main() {
    Admin a;
    a.name = "bob";
    a.level = 2;
    a.describe();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
admin 2, user bob
.EE
.RE
.SH No virtual dispatch
.PP
Which method runs is decided by the type the compiler sees, never at run time. Through a \fBUser *\fR, the call is \fBUser.describe\fR, even when the value is an \fBAdmin\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct User { u8 *name; };
struct Admin : User { i64 level; };

void User.describe() {
    printf("user %s\en", self.name);
}

void Admin.describe() {
    printf("admin %s\en", self.name);
}

i32 main() {
    Admin a;
    a.name = "bob";
    a.describe();
    User *u = &a;
    u.describe();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
admin bob
user bob
.EE
.RE
.PP
For behaviour chosen at run time, put a function pointer in the struct (see Function pointers (see ktm-guide-function-pointers(7))). Each value carries the function it uses:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Shape {
    u8 *name;
    fn(Shape *) f64 area;
};

struct Circle : Shape { f64 r; };
struct Rect : Shape { f64 w, h; };

f64 circleArea(Shape *s) {
    Circle *c = s;
    return 3.14159 * c.r * c.r;
}

f64 rectArea(Shape *s) {
    Rect *r = s;
    return r.w * r.h;
}

i32 main() {
    Circle c;
    c.name = "circle";
    c.area = &circleArea;
    c.r = 1.0;
    Rect r;
    r.name = "rect";
    r.area = &rectArea;
    r.w = 2.0;
    r.h = 3.0;
    Shape *shapes[2] = {&c, &r};
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        Shape *s = shapes[i];
        printf("%s: %.2f\en", s.name, s.area(s));
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
circle: 3.14
rect: 6.00
.EE
.RE
.SH A method is a function
.PP
\fBType.name\fR is an ordinary function whose first parameter is \fBself\fR\&. It can be called that way too, and \fB&Type.name\fR is its address, a \fBfn(Type *, ...)\fR like any other function's:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct User { u8 *name; };

void User.hello() {
    printf("hello %s\en", self.name);
}

void each(User[] users, fn(User *) void f) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < users.len; i++) {
        f(&users[i]);
    }
}

i32 main() {
    User all[2] = {{"ann"}, {"bob"}};
    User.hello(&all[0]);
    each(all, &User.hello);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
hello ann
hello ann
hello bob
.EE
.RE
.PP
There are no bound methods: \fBu.hello\fR without parentheses is an error.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct User { u8 *name; };

void User.hello() {
}

i32 main() {
    User u;
    auto f = u.hello;
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
is a method: call it with `(...)`, or take its address with `&User.hello`
.EE
.RE
.SH Unions
.PP
A union can have methods as well; \fBself\fR points at the union:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
union Bits {
    i64 i;
    f64 f;
};

i64 Bits.exponent() {
    return (self.i >> 52) & 0x7FF;
}

i32 main() {
    Bits b;
    b.f = 8.0;
    printf("%d\en", b.exponent() \- 1023);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3
.EE
.RE
.SH What isn't allowed
.PP
A method can't have the name of one of the struct's fields (inherited ones included):
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x; };

void Point.x() {
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
has a field named `x`: a method cannot have the same name
.EE
.RE
.PP
Calling a field as if it were a method says so:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct Box { i64 len; };

i32 main() {
    Box b;
    return b.len();
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
`len` is a field, not a method: read it without `()`
.EE
.RE
.PP
Only the module that defines a type can give it methods (see Modules and packages (see ktm-guide-modules-and-packages(7))):
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
import json;

void json.Value.dump() {
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
can only be defined in the module that defines it
.EE
.RE
.PP
That goes for \fBString\fR and \fBError\fR too: every program has them, but their methods come from the standard library. A helper of your own for them is a function that takes one:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void String.shout() {
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
methods of `String` can only be defined by the standard library
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void shout(String *s) {
    s.toUpper();
    s.append("!");
}

void main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("hey");
    shout(&s);
    printf("%s\en", s);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
HEY!
.EE
.RE
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
A method is \fBRet Type.name(params) { ... }\fR, written outside the struct, for a struct or a union.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBself\fR is a pointer to the value the method was called on, and can't be reassigned.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBv.name(...)\fR works on a value, a pointer, an array element, a field or a returned struct.
.IP \(bu 2
Default arguments, overloads and varargs work as for functions.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the module that defines a type gives it methods; \fBString\fR\&'s and \fBError\fR\&'s come from the standard library.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstruct D : B\fR extends one struct: \fBD\fR starts with \fBB\fR\&'s fields, has \fBB\fR\&'s methods, and a \fBD *\fR converts to a \fBB *\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
A derived struct's method of the same name hides the base's; \fBB.name(self)\fR calls the base's.
.IP \(bu 2
The call is chosen by the static type: no virtual dispatch. Use function pointers for that.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB&Type.name\fR is the method as a function whose first parameter is \fBself\fR; there are no bound methods.
.IP \(bu 2
A method can't be named like a field, and only the defining module adds methods to a type.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ktm-guide (7),
.BR ktm-guide-structs-and-unions (7),
